+  "displayId": "ISS-001",
+  "id": "ISS-001",
+  "impact": "src/http/handler.ts resolves the request id with `req.headers[\"x-request-id\"] ?? randomUUID()`. `??` only catches null/undefined, so a present-but-empty header (`\"x-request-id\": \"\"`) survives the fallback. Router.dispatch then calls AppLogger.withRequestId(\"\"), which throws \"AppLogger requires a non-empty request id\" (src/platform/logging/AppLogger.ts). A client sending a blank x-request-id therefore gets an unhandled throw out of app.handle() rather than a response. Fix: trim the header and fall back to randomUUID() when it is blank, not only when it is absent.",
+  "location": [
+    "src/http/handler.ts",
+    "src/platform/logging/AppLogger.ts"
+  ],
+  "phase": "p1",
+  "relatedTickets": [
+    "T-4"
+  ],
+  "resolution": "Fixed in T-4. src/http/handler.ts now resolves the request id through `resolveRequestId()`, which trims the header and falls back to randomUUID() when the result is blank, so no path can reach AppLogger.withRequestId with an empty id. T-4 had to close this rather than defer it: its 429 path builds a logger before Router.dispatch, so a rate-limited request with a blank x-request-id would have thrown instead of returning 429. Covered by the handler test \"a blank x-request-id on a rate-limited request still returns 429 rather than throwing\"; the same helper serves the allowed path.",
+  "resolvedDate": "2026-09-20",
+  "severity": "medium",
+  "status": "resolved",
+  "title": "Empty x-request-id header throws instead of falling back to a generated id"
+}
