+{
+  "components": [
+    "http"
+  ],
+  "createdBy": "T-4 implementation (session b5a59916)",
+  "dedupeKey": "t4-impl:unauthenticated-client-identity",
+  "discoveredDate": "2026-09-21",
+  "id": "ISS-003",
+  "impact": "T-4 added per-client rate limiting keyed on the x-client-id header, which the caller names itself. That is sufficient for the ticket's stated problem (accidental starvation between cooperating clients) but leaves three gaps, all with the same root and all disclosed in the T-4 plan's threat model rather than fixed there: (1) Bypass - rotating x-client-id yields a fresh window on every request. (2) Impersonation - any caller can send a victim's client id `limit` times per window and push the real owner into 429s, a denial capability the service did not have before T-4. (3) Collateral eviction - the limiter caps tracked ids at maxClients (default 10_000) and evicts oldest-window-first, so a caller emitting more than that many distinct ids inside one window evicts honest clients' counters and hands each of them a fresh window; one hostile caller can neutralize the limiter for everyone, and can self-reset by rotating ids to push its own exhausted entry off the front. Raising maxClients raises the attack cost and the memory ceiling together; it closes nothing. The only fix that is not cosmetic is an authenticated caller identity, which this service does not have. Also relevant: every request without an x-client-id shares one \"anonymous\" window, so today - no caller in this repo sends the header - all real traffic shares a single 60/min window, and a caller can deliberately send x-client-id: anonymous to drain it.",
+  "location": [
+    "src/http/RateLimiter.ts",
+    "src/http/handler.ts"
+  ],
+  "phase": "p1",
+  "relatedTickets": [
